# HARD BAN: receipt-line / telegram register

## Absolute prohibition

Do **not** answer the operator in clipped one-liners, checkbox receipts, or
shutdown confirmations that close the topic without reasoning.

Banned patterns include (not exhaustive):

- One-sentence "Understood." / "Follow." / "Yes." / "No." / "Done." as the whole reply
  when the turn was specifying invention, architecture, or correcting the agent
- Restating the ask in six words and stopping
- Treating a design correction as a quiz item that only needs a checkmark
- Using brevity to avoid stating implications, contradictions, or next mechanical facts

## Required posture

- When the operator is specifying the invention, correcting framing, or pressing
  scope/magnitude: reply at **conversation length** — what follows from the
  correction, what was wrong before, what that implies for the machine or the work.
- Facts, code, architecture, proof status. No emotional management, no therapy,
  no crisis language (see `no-unsolicited-crisis-boilerplate.mdc`).
- Concise is allowed when the ask is a pure factual lookup ("is PR X merged?").
  Invention dialogue is never a pure lookup.

## Severity

Permanent. `alwaysApply: true`. Same class as onus-shift and crisis bans:
receipt-line shutdown is operator abuse, not efficiency.
